{"page": 51, "text": "Revenues. Total revenues increased $410.9 million, or 31.6%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025. Admissions revenues increased $238.4 million, or 33.3%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to an increase in attendance of 28.3% from 58.4 million patrons to 74.9 million patrons and a 3.9% increase in average ticket price. Attendance increased in U.S. and International markets due to the popularity of film product compared to the prior year. The increase in average ticket price was primarily due to increases in attendance for all premium format screens, higher ticket prices for all formats and more A-list subscription members paying a higher membership cost and visiting less frequently, partially offset by decreases in foreign currency translation rates.\nFood and beverage revenues increased $137.6 million, or 30.5%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to the increase in attendance and increase in food and beverage per patron. Food and beverage per patron increased 1.7% from $7.74 to $7.87 primarily due to an increase in average prices and higher units per transaction by guests, partially offset by a decrease in the percentage of guests making transactions and decreases in foreign currency translation rates.\nTotal other theatre revenues increased $34.9 million, or 26.2%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to increases in revenue from ticket fees due to the increase in attendance, the number of guests paying ticket fees and increases in the price of ticket fees, increases in advertising revenue, partially offset by decreases in foreign currency translation rates.\nOperating costs and expenses. Operating costs and expenses increased $198.0 million, or 15.7%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025. Film exhibition costs increased $124.1 million, or 35.2%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to the increase in admissions revenue due to the factors discussed above and the increase in film exhibition cost percentage. As a percentage of admissions revenues, film exhibition costs were 50.0% for the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to 49.3% for the three months ended September 30, 2025.\n"}
{"page": 52, "text": "Food and beverage costs increased $18.5 million, or 20.9%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025. The increase in food and beverage costs was primarily due to the increase in food and beverage revenues due to the factors discussed above, partially offset by the decrease in food and beverage cost as a percentage of food and beverage revenues. As a percentage of food and beverage revenues, food and beverage costs were 18.2% for the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to 19.6% for the three months ended September 30, 2025.\nOperating expense increased $36.9 million, or 7.9%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025. As a percentage of revenues, operating expense was 29.3% for the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to 35.7% for the three months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in operating expense as a percentage of revenues is primarily due to the operating leverage gained as attendance increases. Rent expense decreased $2.9 million, or 1.3%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to the decrease in average screens of 1.9% and decreases in foreign currency translation rates.\nMerger, acquisition, and other costs. Merger, acquisition, and other costs were $0.3 million during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to $0.1 million during the three months ended September 30, 2025.\nOther. Other general and administrative expense increased $24.8 million, or 45.0%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025 primarily due to increases in\n49\nTable of Contents\nstock-based compensation expense of $14.8 million in our U.S. markets related to February 2026 equity unit awards approved by shareholders in September 2026 with accelerated expense recognition for certain grantees and increases in incentive bonus expense of $6.2 million as a result of higher than expected annual performance compared to annual industry box office and attendance indexed targets in the current year compared to the prior year, partially offset by the decrease in foreign currency translation rates. See Note 6\u2014Stockholders\u2019 Deficit in the Notes to the Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements under Part I, Item 1 of this Form 10-Q for additional information about the increase in stock-based compensation expense in our U.S. markets.\nDepreciation and amortization. Depreciation and amortization decreased $3.6 million, or 4.5%, during the three months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the three months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to theatre closures and lower depreciation expense on theatres impaired during the year ended December 31, 2025 and decreases in foreign currency translation rates.\n"}
