{"page": 67, "text": "Income tax provision. The income tax provision was $15.1 million and $4.0 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and September 30, 2025, respectively. See Note 7\u2014Income Taxes in the Notes to the Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements under Part I, Item 1 of this Form 10-Q for further information.\nNet loss. Net loss was $19.2 million and $505.0 million during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, and September 30, 2025, respectively. Net loss during the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to net loss for the nine months ended September 30, 2025 was positively impacted by the increase in attendance as a result of the popularity of new film releases compared to the prior year, decreases in other expense, increases in investment income, and decreases in depreciation and amortization, partially offset by increases in general and administrative expenses, increases in interest expense, increases in income tax provision, increases in rent expense and increases in foreign currency translation rates.\nTheatrical Exhibition\u2014U.S. Markets\nRevenues. Total revenues increased $561.4 million, or 20.5%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025. Admissions revenues increased $296.6 million, or 20.1%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to an increase in attendance of 16.6% from 116.1 million patrons to 135.4 million patrons and a 2.9% increase in average ticket price. Attendance increased in U.S. markets due to the popularity of film product compared to the prior year. The increase in average ticket price was primarily due to increased ticket prices for all formats and increases in attendance for IMAX, PLF and XL screens, partially offset by decreases in contributions from 3D screens.\nFood and beverage revenues increased $200.6 million, or 20.3%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to the increase in attendance and increase\n56\nTable of Contents\nin food and beverage per patron. Food and beverage per patron increased 3.0% from $8.53 to $8.79 primarily due to an increase in average prices, partially offset by lower units per transaction by guests.\n"}
{"page": 68, "text": "Total other theatre revenues increased $64.2 million, or 23.5%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to increases in revenue from ticket fees due to the increase in attendance, the number of guests paying ticket fees and increases in the price of ticket fees and increases in advertising revenue. As a result of our Amended ESA, advertising revenue increased from the prior year by $5.2 million due to an increase in discount rates related to the significant financing component of the Amended ESA, partially offset by lower amortization of deferred revenues due to an increase in the term of the Amended ESA. See Note 3\u2014Revenue Recognition in the Notes to the Condensed Consolidated Financial Statements under Part I, Item 1 of this Form 10-Q for additional information about the Amended ESA.\nOperating costs and expenses. Operating costs and expenses increased $220.3 million, or 8.2%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025. Film exhibition costs increased $160.6 million, or 21.1%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to the increase in admissions revenue due to the factors discussed above. As a percentage of admissions revenues, film exhibition costs were 52.1% for the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to 51.7% for the nine months ended September 30, 2025.\nFood and beverage costs increased $19.7 million, or 11.0%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The increase in food and beverage costs was primarily due to the increase in food and beverage revenues due to the factors discussed above, partially offset by the decrease in food and beverage cost as a percentage of food and beverage revenues. As a percentage of food and beverage revenues, food and beverage costs were 16.7% for the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to 18.1% for the nine months ended September 30, 2025.\nOperating expense increased by $33.8 million, or 3.4%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025. As a percentage of revenues, operating expense was 30.9% for the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to 36.0% for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in operating expense as a percentage of revenues is primarily due to the operating leverage gained as attendance increases. Rent expense decreased $5.4 million, or 1.1%, during the nine months ended September 30, 2026, compared to the nine months ended September 30, 2025, primarily due to a decrease in average screens of 2.0%.\n"}
